WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.300
Film Courage: LaKeith, I understand you used
Google searches to break into acting?

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.520
LaKeith Stanfield: Yeah…I just got out of
high school, trying to figure out what I want

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.530
to do.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:17.740
Well, I kind of knew what I wanted to do,
I wanted to act in some capacity.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:24.289
So I started Googling acting schools, acting
stuff, anything that encompassed acting, trying

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:26.570
to find a channel in.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:33.460
I just Googled everything and pages and pages
of things popped up and then I just filled

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.969
everything out and then eventually people
started answering.

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:42.190
And a lot of those were scams and things where
I would ultimately end up wasting my time.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.460
So I ended up going back and forth to Victorville,
scrounging up change.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.629
One time it was a school for modeling and
career center?

00:00:50.629 --> 00:00:56.520
And so they had us get up on the runway, taught
us to walk the runway.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.260
And taught us all the names of the brands,
Fendi, Gucci, Prada.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.629
They were names that were of no use to me
really.

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:07.490
But at the time I realized every other week
they had agents come in for people who were

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.560
inspired to get into acting.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.110
So I wanted to get in front of one of those
agents.

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:15.579
And when one of those agents came through
“I want to go in!”

00:01:15.579 --> 00:01:21.750
Every audition day was Thursday and they said
“Well, do you have anything prepared?”

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.030
And I said “Yes.”

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.189
I didn’t, but I went in anyway.

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:30.160
And I just did the first thing that came to
my mind which was to jump up on the chair

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.850
and be like…and do some random stuff.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:42.640
And the guy saw something in me that he liked
and he signed me to his commercial agency.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.780
And there I learned what it meant to do auditions
and fail and learn what it meant to get rejected

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:53.450
but be in the room and improve my comfortability
over time.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.119
So it would prove to be a very important chapter.

00:01:56.119 --> 00:01:59.880
Although at the time I thought I was just
wasting my time.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:03.469
It proved to be really important once I got
out there and started auditioning.

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:07.830
I felt comfortable because I’d been rejected
and I had been through it all already and

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.170
there was nothing left to fear.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.600
So yeah…that was what came of the Internet.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.070
Film Courage: And I understand you didn’t
have a car while you were in Victorville?

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.070
LaKeith Stanfield: No.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:21.240
Film Courage: And from what I know I don’t
think the bus really runs there from Los Angeles?

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.240
LaKeith Stanfield: No.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.240
There’s a train that runs there.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.930
You’ve got to scramble up some change and
take the train.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:30.430
Film Courage: Okay, but that sounds like it’s
several stops.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:33.500
So how are you going on auditions?

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.670
Or even to this modeling class/school and
coming back [to Victorville]?

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:38.670
That sounds like a lot.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:43.320
LaKeith Stanfield: Half of it was just trying
to hustle up money just to get down on the

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:44.320
train.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.420
And some of it was my parents.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:53.980
They would help me every now and again to
take me, whenever my bugging them to go…”Fine..Whatever.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.170
Again?

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.360
For what?”

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.850
Because when you are beginning auditioning
you don’t realize how many rejections involved

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:01.850
in it.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.920
So you get rejected 20, 30 times and you are
like “What am I still doing this for?

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.190
Obviously it’s not going to work.”

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:10.470
And it was so much work for us to get down
there, they were just like “This is becoming

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.590
more of an expense than anything.”

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:17.050
And so I had to figure out ways to get down
there on my own after awhile.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.910
And I missed a lot of auditions because I
couldn’t make it.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.700
And that was sad because at the time I hated
missing them.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.460
I would rather get rejected than to miss it,
you know?

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:33.260
Film Courage: What’s your advice for another
actor that maybe they don’t have the formal

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:37.560
training but they know they really want to
do this?

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.560
What would you advise them?

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:39.560
What would they be Googling?

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.060
LaKeith Stanfield: I don’t know?

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:47.430
Now there is probably 900 pages when I Googled
acting, probably 9 million now.

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.560
So there’s much more to weed from.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.560
Much more opportunity to get caught up in
different things.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:59.250
I would say stay in school, stay diligent,
go through high school, go through college,

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:03.680
and try to look through opportunities after
having sort of learned all you can learn and

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.290
situate yourself and be comfortable before
you get into acting.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:10.000
Know that there is something else that you
could be doing.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.540
Know that you have a back-up plan because
this is a very difficult, tough game to get

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:14.540
into.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.950
And it’s tough to maintain even once you’re
in.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:20.950
It’s not like just because you booked something
you are going to be working forever.

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.130
So you always have to keep that hustle up
and continue to look for new avenues and know

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.050
that if this doesn’t work, you’ve got
something else in the back and if that don’t

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.300
work, you got something else.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.770
You have other alternatives, as well.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:35.420
And don’t focus all of your energy on acting
would be my thing.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:41.080
Film Courage: Did you ever feel pushback from
people, from even other actors who maybe were

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:45.610
classically trained and they saw you booking
more work and they were like “Well, you

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.760
didn’t try this technique…”

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.350
And you could feel that, maybe it wasn’t
even spoken?

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.550
LaKeith Stanfield: Oh yeah.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.550
Totally.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:56.760
It wasn’t spoken and it was spoken in certain
parts.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.500
But everybody’s got their particular route.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.660
One thing I think is important to being an
actor is always keep an open mind, always

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:08.870
be willing to learn, and don’t reach a point
where you feel like I’ve learned enough

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.590
and now I don’t need to know anything else.

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:13.750
Because your talent will take you so far but
you need to be constantly trying to develop

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:19.400
skills and learning how to access these characters
and analyze these stories and tell them in

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.440
the most authentic way.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:24.641
And while your talent will take you a long
way, it won’t take you to the heights that

00:05:24.641 --> 00:05:27.820
you want to go because there’s a cap, a
moment where you’re like “Well, this is

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:29.240
just about my talent.”

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:34.380
You need to constantly stretch yourself, travel,
meet new people, engage in new experiences

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:40.320
(fearlessly) and sort of download that information
into creating what you want to create.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.390
Every role is different, so what may work
for one role, may not work for another and

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:45.390
vice versa.

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.330
So sometimes your talent will be great for
a certain role and then for another role you

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:54.820
have to reach outside the boundaries of your
natural talent in order to tap into it.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:59.580
Those are things that I find the most rewarding
and the most difficult and they cause me to

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.970
have to get studious and learn all of these
things, you know?

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:06.260
And get into these different lifestyles and
understanding.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:10.410
So always keep an open mind and I think you
can explore and do whatever you need to do.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.760
And realize, remember this game is not a competition.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.290
It seems like a competition, but it’s not.

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:18.130
The only person you’re in competition with
is yourself.

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.250
If you think you can’t do a certain thing,
you can’t.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.360
If you think you can, you can.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:22.770
You’ve just got to push yourself.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:28.100
Film Courage: Have there ever been roles that
you don’t want to take, for whatever reason?

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.990
You don’t believe in the project or there
is something about the character that you

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.380
just wouldn’t feel good about playing?

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:35.770
LaKeith Stanfield: Totally, yeah.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.590
Characters are realizations from people’s
imaginations.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:44.320
So naturally some of are going to fit with
your imagination and some aren’t.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.650
And some are based off of real people and
are accurate depictions and some aren’t.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:52.680
So you have to use discernment to figure out
what speaks to you, what’s real to you.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:56.669
I think when you first start you are going
to take anything that comes at you because

00:06:56.669 --> 00:06:57.919
you’re trying to work, that’s understandable.

00:06:57.919 --> 00:06:59.530
That’s what I did.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.580
I think most actors do that when they are
first starting out because they need to work

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:04.140
because it’s a job.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:10.700
But once you start to develop the craft you
can weed out what speaks to you and what is

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:11.700
important.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:15.280
Because it’s also important to do things
that correlate with your frequency, that go

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.190
with how you are attempting to move.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:19.870
Because if it doesn’t, you are portraying
something that isn’t genuine.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:23.270
And then your performance, people are going
to see it and say “There’s something that

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.480
isn’t genuine about that?”

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:26.240
So make sure it connects to you.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:31.180
This is why I think it’s important to expand
your horizon and awareness so then more things

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:36.250
have the opportunity to click with you.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:38.500
The more broad your horizon, the more chance
you are able to say “Oh, yeah.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:39.810
I know what that is.”

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.280
Rather than living in a sort of vacuum.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.139
If something is coming into your awareness,
you might be like “Hmmm, that’s too foreign.

00:07:44.139 --> 00:07:46.530
I don’t know what that is.”

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:48.670
And never judge the character.

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:52.050
Film Courage: What did Colin teach you about
life?

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.470
LaKeith Stanfield: Colin Stark?

00:07:54.470 --> 00:08:01.650
Playing him [Colin Warner from CROWN HEIGHTS]
in this movie he sort of reestablished the

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:13.310
idea to persevere in the face of insurmountable
odds, continue to push forward and remain

00:08:13.310 --> 00:08:15.360
steadfast in what you believe in.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.490
And the more you do that, the more you may
realize what you were attempting to realize.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:24.280
Break the chains of whatever chains that are
on you…there are physical chains and psychological

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.280
chains.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.090
Stay the course and you can eventually break
them.

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:30.870
Film Courage: Did you feel pressure playing
someone who was very much still alive, knowing

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:33.750
that the film would mean very much to them?

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.820
LaKeith Stanfield: To an extent, yes.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.000
I think that I was lulled by a great team
that I was around.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:45.060
Colin himself being in it helped a lot and
made me feel that he’s with us in this,

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:46.370
so I’m at ease.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:50.290
He believes in the story, he believes in the
ability, so we’re just going to move forward

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:51.570
in the spirit of that.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.020
Also, I was blessed by his family.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:59.210
He took me into their house and blessed me
at this little…sort of like…shrine thing

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:00.370
where they blessed me.

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:03.760
So I felt like I got the spirit…so we just
went from there.

